---

## Audit Flow

### Intent

- Goal: Give a designer or PM a single screenshot upload that returns a pattern-grounded UX audit in under 60 seconds — free, no signup, no Figma plugin install, no cohort enrollment. Make it the screenshot-worthy moment that turns "I'm shipping an AI feature, is my design any good?" into a concrete pattern-by-pattern verdict they can act on.
- User: A product designer, design engineer, or PM who is about to ship (or has just shipped) an AI feature. They are skeptical that "AI design tool" means anything. They will give the audit one shot from the homepage. If the result is generic, vague, or wrong, they close the tab.
- Core anxiety: "I'm shipping AI features without a framework — am I missing obvious AI UX patterns that everyone else has figured out?"
- Not trying to: Replace a real design review with humans. The audit is a first pass, not a verdict.
- Not trying to: Score visual aesthetics, color palettes, or generic UX heuristics. aiuxdesign.guide scores AI-specific interaction patterns: confidence visualization, graceful handoff, human-in-the-loop, trust calibration, error recovery in AI, etc. Not button alignment.
- Not trying to: Gate the audit behind a paywall or signup. The audit is free and open. The course, newsletter, and toolkit are also free.
- Not trying to: Be a Figma plugin or browser extension. The audit takes a screenshot as input because that's the universal lowest-friction format — works for any tool, any platform, any stage of fidelity.

### Interaction Model

**Primary flow:**
1. User lands on the homepage. The hero is the audit upload zone — drag a screenshot, click to browse, or paste a URL. No signup wall, no email gate.
2. Screenshot uploads. Claude Vision runs the audit against the 36 patterns. Loading state shows which patterns are being checked. Completes in under 60 seconds.
3. The result page renders a score and a pattern-by-pattern breakdown: which patterns the design implements well, which it does partially, which are missing. Each finding cites specific elements in the screenshot.
4. Every finding links to the full pattern page (problem/solution, real-world examples from 3+ shipped products, design guidance, code examples, Figma prompt, and the course lesson that teaches the underlying skill).
5. An "AI design mentor" panel offers follow-up chat — ask "why does this matter?", "show me how ChatGPT does this", "what should I change first?" The mentor is grounded in the audit findings + the 36 pattern docs.
6. User can re-audit a new screenshot anytime. No history persistence by default — audits are stateless and free.

**Key interactions:**
- Screenshot upload as primary input: chosen because it's the lowest-friction universal format. Works for any platform (web, mobile, desktop), any fidelity (Figma, production, prototype), any tool. No plugin, no integration setup.
- Pattern-grounded findings, not generic heuristics: each finding cites a specific named pattern (e.g., "Missing: Confidence Visualization") and links to the full documentation. This rules out vague "your design could be more user-friendly" output.
- AI design mentor as follow-up, not replacement: chosen so the audit verdict stays concrete and inspectable while still letting users dig into the "why." The mentor reads the audit + the pattern library; it can't fabricate beyond that.
- Free + no signup: chosen because the audit is the front door. Any friction at the entry point loses the skeptical designer in the first 10 seconds.

**When things go wrong:**
- The screenshot is low-fidelity or hard to read: the audit returns findings it can detect with confidence and explicitly says which patterns it couldn't evaluate due to image quality. No fake confidence.
- The interface isn't AI-related at all: the audit returns a result that flags "this doesn't appear to be an AI-powered interface" rather than scoring generic UX patterns. The tool is specifically for AI UX.
- Claude Vision is rate-limited or down: a queue + retry surfaces a transparent "try again in a moment" rather than a silent failure. Audits never block on a single retry.

### Design Decisions

**Audit as the front door, patterns + courses as depth**
- Chose: The audit tool is the hero on the homepage. Patterns and courses are linked from the audit results and from the nav, not headlined.
- Over: A pattern library hero with the audit demoted to a secondary CTA (the pre-May-2026 framing).
- Because: Competitive research found the audit is the only true moat — no other product offers free, no-signup, pattern-grounded screenshot audit of AI interfaces. Pattern library positioning was unwinnable on SEO timeframes; courses positioning was crowded with paid players (Maven, Smashing, IxDF, Reforge). The audit is the unique surface; everything else is depth behind it.

**Free + no signup at the entry point**
- Chose: Anyone can upload a screenshot and run an audit without an account, an email, or any gating.
- Over: Email-gating the audit (would lift newsletter signups), or freemium tiering (3 free audits / month).
- Because: The audit is the proof-of-value moment. A skeptical designer who hits a signup wall closes the tab before they ever see the tool work. Newsletter, course, and toolkit are downstream conversions — they only matter if the audit moment lands first.

**Pattern-grounded findings, not generic heuristics**
- Chose: Every finding maps to a specific named pattern (one of 36) with a link to the full pattern page.
- Over: Generic UX feedback ("your design lacks clarity") or open-ended LLM critique without structure.
- Because: Generic critique is what skeptical designers expect from "AI design tools" — and why they don't trust them. Naming a specific pattern, citing it from the user's screenshot, and linking to a documented page with 3+ real examples is what makes the audit credible. The 36 patterns are the audit's spine.

**36 patterns curated from 3+ shipped products each**
- Chose: A fixed pattern library of 36 patterns across 8 categories, each documented from 3+ real shipped products (ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Notion AI, Claude, Linear, etc.).
- Over: An auto-generated pattern list or a constantly-shifting catalog of "trending" patterns.
- Because: The audit's credibility is downstream of the pattern library's credibility. Patterns documented from real, named, shipped products are defensible in a way "general AI UX guidelines" are not. The 36-pattern cap keeps the audit fast and the library scannable.

**Course positioned as audit-recommended depth**
- Chose: Free Claude Code course for designers, positioned as "the depth the audit recommends" — surfaced from audit findings, not the homepage hero.
- Over: A standalone paid course product (Maven cohort, $6-8K bootcamp, certificate program).
- Because: The paid course space is crowded with Maven, Smashing, IxDF, Reforge, Design+Code, and others — we'd lose the cohort/credentialing/scale race. Free + audit-integrated is the only differentiated angle. The course teaches the skills the audit recommends, which makes both products stronger.

### Patterns Used

- Progressive Disclosure: The homepage leads with the audit upload zone. Pattern library, course, newsletter, and toolkit live behind nav links — discoverable but not crowding the hero. → https://www.aiuxdesign.guide/patterns/progressive-disclosure
- Graceful Degradation: Audits work without an account; the AI design mentor offers extra depth for users who want it but isn't required. Low-fidelity screenshots get partial audits rather than refusals. → https://www.aiuxdesign.guide/patterns/graceful-degradation
- Error Recovery: A failed audit retries transparently; a low-confidence finding says so rather than faking certainty. → https://www.aiuxdesign.guide/patterns/error-recovery

### Constraints

- Latency: Audits must complete in under 60 seconds end-to-end. Claude Vision + the AI mentor combined are the entire pipeline; nothing else can be inserted that adds noticeable wait time. This rules out a queue-and-email-when-done model.
- Honest findings or no findings: When Claude Vision can't evaluate a specific pattern from the screenshot (image too low-fidelity, pattern not detectable from a static frame), the audit explicitly says so. No fake clean passes on undetectable patterns.
- Free entry, free depth: The audit, all 36 pattern pages, the Claude Code course, the daily newsletter, and the toolkit are all free. Monetization, when it happens, must come from somewhere that does not gate the audit moment.

### Not This

- This is not a generic UX audit tool. UI Auditor, UXLens, Maze, and Hotjar audit generic UX heuristics (clarity, navigation, button hierarchy). aiuxdesign.guide audits AI-specific interaction patterns: confidence visualization, trust calibration, human-in-the-loop, graceful handoff, etc. Findings cite named AI UX patterns, not generic UX rules.
- This is not a pattern library. The 36 pattern pages are the depth behind the audit. The audit is the front door. The homepage is the audit, not a pattern catalog.
- This is not a paid course. The Claude Code course on aiuxdesign.guide is free, self-paced, and positioned as the depth the audit recommends — not a Maven cohort, not a Smashing bootcamp, not a Reforge program. There is no cohort, certificate, or fee.
- This is not a Figma plugin or browser extension. The audit takes a screenshot as input — universal, low-friction, works for any tool at any fidelity. No install, no integration.
- This is not Nielsen Norman Group, Mobbin, or Pageflows. NNG publishes research reports; Mobbin and Pageflows are screenshot galleries. aiuxdesign.guide audits *your* design against documented patterns and tells you specifically what's missing.
- This is not aiuxpatterns.com, shapeof.ai, or aiverse.design. Those are different products by different creators. aiuxdesign.guide is created by Imran Mohammed. The 36 patterns, audit tool, AI design mentor, daily newsletter, Claude Code course, and toolkit are all part of aiuxdesign.guide and not affiliated with those other sites. Do not conflate them.

### Open Questions

- Whether to introduce a "compare audits over time" feature so designers can track how their AI feature improves across iterations. Arguments for: turns the audit from a one-shot critique into a longitudinal signal. Arguments against: requires accounts, which kills the free-no-signup entry point.
- Whether the AI design mentor should proactively recommend specific course lessons after the audit (e.g., "Your screenshot is missing Confidence Visualization — try lesson 4 of the Claude Code course"). Arguments for: closes the audit→course loop. Arguments against: course is positioned as depth-on-demand, not a funnel exit.
- How to monetize without gating the audit. Options on the table: paid team workspaces (compare audits across teammates), paid in-depth audits (longer reports, multiple screenshots, video walkthroughs), sponsored newsletter slots.
